hi there steve kaufman here  um today i was going to answer more of  these questions that i received  as part of our sort of competition what  question would you like to ask steve if  you have 30 minute conversation  and i was going over some of them and i  will go over some of them  but it dawned on me that there's a  certain common theme  throughout  you know the answers that i want to give  and so really the subject of my little  chat today is the importance of passive  vocabulary  um  in all of the languages that i have  learned  uh you know if i if i look for sort of a  measurement of how i'm doing if i look  for a goal  you know other than the fact that i like  to read things that are interesting and  listen to things that are interesting  my goal is to increase my passive  vocabulary  and  i firmly believe that the larger your  passive vocabulary the greater your  potential in the language and so that  the simplest  measure of where you are in a language  is how many words you  know or understand even  just in context  now  right away there are people who say you  know that our known word counted link is  not accurate  because  you know  we measure each form of the word as a  different word that's true so there's no  scientific value i can't go bragging  that i know  90 000 words in russian when in fact in  terms of word families it might only be  30 000 and then there are people who say  there aren't that many words in the  dictionary and they it doesn't matter  none of that matters the only thing that  matters is that i am motivated to  increase my known word count increase my  passive vocabulary  and  i think that to me if i look at some of  the questions here you know  uh how can i um what  what is the effective way to learn a  bunch of languages do a lot of listening  and reading and increase your passive  vocabulary you can't  be in a position to speak them every day  all of them if you're learning a number  of languages but if you have a large  passive vocabulary in a number of  languages when you do get that  opportunity  you can take advantage and you can  develop your speaking quite quickly i  have had the experience many times when  i build up my passive vocabulary beyond  a certain point and especially if i  combine listening and reading there are  phrases and words in my brain when i  start to speak it's difficult but very  quickly i start to be able to use these  words i activate my passive vocabulary  and remember the native speaker always  has a much larger active vocabulary than  you the learner therefore you need to be  able to understand what people are  saying  so  you know the answer is how do i motivate  myself how about you set yourself some  goals of where you want to get to in  terms your in terms of your passive  vocabulary your your known words count  on link or whatever so that you can pick  up a book and you can watch a movie and  you can understand the language because  if you do those things  when the opportunity to speak arrives  and eventually as i say all the time we  need to speak a lot  you will have the weapons you'll have  the tools you'll be able to manage  so you know uh  how to be motivated seems to be a common  theme okay you have to be motivated by  the interest but you can also again set  yourself this target of increasing your  passive vocabulary question what was  your catalyst for starting to learn  languages  i just got interested in french then i  was sent to learn chinese and since then  i once you realize that you can learn  languages that is something that you can  do  and that you can then discover a new  world like i happen to see something on  twitter that was written in arabic i  don't know the words but i can make out  the sounds more or less because the  arabic writing system is kind of more or  less and so far as how it's it's  pronounced  so the motivation it feeds on itself but  here again if you're on your first  language  dra and you aren't yet able to consume  interesting  to you content or you can't understand  movies or  then drive that passive vocabulary word  count  uh  you know for example if i want to call  it  which audiobook should i start with well  that depends entirely on your interest i  can't possibly answer that question  uh how to select material again you have  to scan you have to search the internet  you can for example if you're looking  for as i did and say ukrainian or  romanian i simply in google translate if  i can't yet say ebook on ukrainian  history i'll put it in english get it in  ukrainian or romania and put it into  google  and up come some sites and then i choose  something that i want and i download it  and use it but that's secondary so you  there are these different ways if you  are genuinely motivated you will find  ways to select material of interest you  will find the content that you want the  audiobooks the radio programs you have  to be motivated to do it the motivation  comes from your interest but  as a sort of measurable  milestones that you target  work on your passive vocabulary that's  my advice  uh and and people sort of  i think in traditional language  instruction they're too concerned about  what you're able to say it doesn't  matter what you're able to say at the  beginning if you can introduce yourself  or if you know the names of uncle  cousin nephew or whatever they teach and  then the different colors and stuff  which you're going to forget anyway  it doesn't much matter what you're able  to say at the beginning what matters is  are you eventually going to be able to  engage with the language in a meaningful  way and in order to do that you have to  have a large passive vocabulary so  my advice and in a way my answer to a  lot of these questions is focus on your  passive vocabulary  thank you for listening and i look  forward to your comments  [Music]  you
 

